From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 00:55:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] bind dark mode to the app's own toggle; stamp every document;
 close the loop
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THE COLOUR BUG WAS ONE MISSING LINE
-----------------------------------
index.css declared no `@custom-variant dark`, so Tailwind v4's `dark:`
variant still meant `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` — the operating
system — while ThemeContext writes a `.dark` class on <html>. The app was
being styled by two different sources at once:

  CSS custom properties (--background, --card, --muted-foreground)
      switch on the .dark CLASS, i.e. the toggle
  every `dark:` utility, ~300 of them
      switched on the OS SETTING

On a machine with the OS in dark mode and the app in light mode — the
default for anyone who has never touched the toggle — you get white rows
carrying dark-mode badge colours. Reproduced exactly: with `theme class:
light` and colorScheme dark, "offen" rendered dark:bg-amber-900/30 at 30 %
opacity with dark:text-amber-300, i.e. pale yellow text on a near-white
row, and "nicht erforderlich" rendered dark:bg-slate-800, a near-black
pill in a white table. Both are in the screenshots that prompted this.

check-ui.mjs now runs the entire audit across the four combinations of OS
scheme x app theme. Verified it catches the bug it was written for: with
the variant removed it reports 23 contrast failures including a literal
1:1 — white text on white — that no one could see at all.

61 surfaces clean across all four themes.

EVERY GENERATED DOCUMENT IS DATED AND TIMED
-------------------------------------------
shared/generated-stamp.ts is the single source: `22.08.2026, 14:07 Uhr`
for print, `2026-08-22_1407` for filenames, Europe/Berlin explicitly,
because a stamp read months later is only meaningful if everyone agrees
which 14:07 it was.

The Checkliste export printed the date only, so two exports of the same
project on one day produced two identical filenames and two documents a
reader could not tell apart — the second silently replaced the first in
the download folder. Checkliste, Projektblatt and the CSV export all
carry date and time now, in the document and in the filename.

THE THIRD ACTION: PROJEKTBLATT
------------------------------
Beside "Alle Projekte dieser Station" and "Bahnhofsmanagement", the
detail dialog now prints the project as an A4 sheet: Stammdaten, all 14
Fachprüfungen with status, Prüfer, Prüfdatum and the address the app
would write to, the Kommentar and the Projektlink.

The stamp is deliberately prominent rather than a footnote — under the
title AND in the footer of every page — because a project sheet is a
snapshot of live data and the reader has to be able to date the paper in
their hand without hunting. Verified end to end: the button downloads
Projektblatt_G.011540063_Langenselbold_2026-08-22_0226.pdf, 19.8 kB,
embedded Inter, `pdffonts` reports `uni yes` so it is searchable.

It is its own 5.4 kB lazy chunk; the ~400 kB PDF engine still never
reaches the entry chunk, which moved 431.65 -> 431.70 kB.

OUTLOOK AND TEAMS GET A WRITTEN MESSAGE
---------------------------------------
Every contact button produced a subject and an EMPTY BODY. The recipient
got "Projekt G.011540063 – Langenselbold" and a blank page, the sender
retyped the context they were just looking at, and nothing said where the
message came from, so a reply landed in a thread with no way back.

shared/message.ts builds both channels from one context: Projektnummer,
Station, Bahnhofsmanagement, Projektstand, Beschreibung, Termin, and —
when the message is about one Gewerk — that Fachprüfung's status, Prüfer
and Prüfdatum. It closes with a provenance block naming the app, the
moment, and a real deep link back to the record (`/projects?q=<Nr>`,
which the Projekte page seeds its search from).

A Teams chat has no subject field, so the message opens with the subject
line; otherwise the recipient sees a body with no idea which project it
names. Fields that are empty are omitted rather than filled with a
placeholder: a prefilled mail that states something false is worse than a
short one.

ANMELDUNG NOW FINISHES THE PROCESS
----------------------------------
The wizard created the project and the review rows and stopped. Nothing
told the departments a Prüfung was waiting — which is what the Excel
macro this replaces existed to do. The confirmation screen now offers one
prefilled Outlook mail per open Fachbereich, addressed from Hilfsdatei,
and names the ones with no address on file instead of opening an empty
mail (LST has neither of its two rows filled in).

And "Zur Projektübersicht" went to all 1,298 projects. It is now "Projekt
öffnen" and lands on /projects?q=<Projektnummer> — the record the user
just spent five steps filling in.

ALSO
----
The navigation gate asserted a <table> existed 900ms after clicking, and
/projects parses 1,298 projects and 18,172 review rows out of 3.6 MB of
JSON before one exists. It failed on a 2-core runner for slowness rather
than for a defect; it waits for the marker now.

The wizard-submit check silently falls back to the draft path when the
submit button is disabled. It now says so out loud — a check that
degrades to a weaker assertion reads as a pass for the thing it stopped
testing. Confirmed the submit branch runs, so the notification and
landing assertions above are really exercised.

Gates: 0 type errors · 168 passed / 6 skipped (15 files) · 72 lint
warnings (unchanged) · e2e 37/37 · responsive 61/61 · map 6/6 · ui 61/61
across four theme combinations.
